Figure 8.2: Sentinel-2 true-colour summer composite for the London study area used in the practical.











Figure 8.3: Training polygons for the five supervised classes used to sample Sentinel-2 pixels in London.






var rf = ee.Classifier.smileRandomForest({
  numberOfTrees: 100,
  seed: 7
}).train({
  features: training,
  classProperty: 'class',
  inputProperties: predictors.bandNames()
});

var classified = predictors.classify(rf);
var validated = validation.classify(rf);
var testCM = validated.errorMatrix('class', 'classification');






Figure 8.4: Random forest land-cover classification of London from Sentinel-2. The strongest pattern is the dense urban core along the Thames, while the suburban fringe looks more mixed and patchy because many pixels combine vegetation and built surfaces.









Figure 8.5: Accuracy assessment from the Google Earth Engine console. The reported validation accuracy is high, but should be interpreted cautiously because the train-test split was done at pixel level within the sampled polygons.









Figure 8.6: A clearer example of how a confusion matrix can be read alongside overall accuracy and class-wise user’s and producer’s accuracy. This is much closer to the logic behind the Earth Engine output used in the practical. Source: Wikimedia Commons (Qiongjx, 2025).

Figure 9.1: A generic illustration of object-based image analysis, where neighbouring pixels are grouped into larger units before interpretation. Source: Wikimedia Commons (Uddinkabir, 2015).











Figure 9.2: A simple mixed-pixel diagram. A single pixel can contain several land-cover types at once, which is exactly why hard urban classifications can be misleading. Source: Wikimedia Commons (Beck, 2012).
















Figure 9.3: SNIC segmentation of the London Sentinel-2 composite. The point here is not that every object is “correct”, but that the image is no longer being treated as a simple pixel grid.











Figure 9.4: Object-level NDVI calculated from the SNIC output. The smoother pattern is useful because it reduces some of the visual noise seen in pixel-based vegetation measures.







var snic = ee.Algorithms.Image.Segmentation.SNIC({
  image: predictors,
  compactness: 1,
  connectivity: 8,
  neighborhoodSize: 60,
  seeds: ee.Algorithms.Image.Segmentation.seedGrid(30, 'hex')
});

var objectNdvi = snic.normalizedDifference(['B8_mean', 'B4_mean']).rename('obj_ndvi');
var objectImage = ee.Image.cat([
  snic.select(['B2_mean', 'B3_mean', 'B4_mean', 'B8_mean', 'B11_mean', 'B12_mean']),
  objectNdvi
]).float();






Figure 9.5: Object-based random forest classification of London. Compared with week 7, the main gain is not a radically different class pattern but a smoother and more interpretable surface.









Figure 9.6: Accuracy output from the object-based classification. The reported overall accuracy is extremely high, but I treat that as a sign of spatial dependence and repeated object values rather than strong independent validation.

Figure 10.1: NASA example showing how built surfaces retain more heat than vegetated areas. It is a simple image, but it makes the basic physical logic of urban heat islands very clear. Source: NASA Science (NASA Science, 2025).










Figure 10.2: NASA Landsat example for New York City showing vegetation on the left and temperature on the right. It is a useful reminder that greener surfaces often line up with cooler temperatures, which connects this week’s heat analysis back to the NDVI and land-cover work from earlier weeks. Source: Robert Simmon using Landsat data (Simmon, 2025).













Figure 10.3: Mean summer land surface temperature in London derived from Landsat 9. The main advantage here is spatial detail: local thermal contrasts are much clearer than in a coarse product.











Figure 10.4: Mean summer land surface temperature in London derived from merged MODIS Aqua and Terra observations. The 1 km pixels smooth away much of the fine urban structure, but the product is much better for repeated monitoring through the summer.













Figure 10.5: London summer land surface temperature time series from merged MODIS Aqua and Terra daytime observations. The sharp same-day jumps reflect the fact that the two sensors pass at different times rather than a single smoothed daily value.



var landsatLstMean = landsat.select('LST_C').mean().clip(greaterLondon);

var modisMerged = modisAqua.merge(modisTerra).sort('system:time_start');

var boroughLandsatMean = landsatLstMean.reduceRegions({
  collection: londonBoroughs,
  reducer: ee.Reducer.mean(),
  scale: 30
});






Figure 10.6: Ranking of mean summer LST by London borough from the Landsat results. The chart is easier to read than the choropleth map and shows clearly that average heat exposure is not evenly distributed across the city.
